WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:03.810
Hank: Good morning, John, I have developed
an unhealthy obsession with the word 'cool'.

00:00:03.810 --> 00:00:08.452
Not the concept of 'cool', that's just--I
mean--that's a lot and I'm clearly not an

00:00:08.452 --> 00:00:09.452
expert.

00:00:09.452 --> 00:00:15.139
I mean the word or words that we use to identify
that mutable mix of simultaneously embodying

00:00:15.139 --> 00:00:19.860
and defying culture and defining culture as
well, if nothing else, cool and its neighborhood

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:24.270
of words attempt to define a leading edge
of cultures' aesthetic, an edge that seems

00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:27.740
out of everyone's control, precisely because
it's controlled by everyone, and there are

00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:29.869
a lot of these words precisely for that reason.

00:00:29.869 --> 00:00:33.730
As culture changes, it needs new words to
embody itself, because those old words are

00:00:33.730 --> 00:00:38.020
defining an old culture, and as culture changes
faster, we get more and more of them.

00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:41.510
Now, it's widely accepted that the word with
the most synonyms in English is drunk.

00:00:41.510 --> 00:00:45.470
This was, I think, first proposed by Benjamin
Franklin, and it is difficult to argue with,

00:00:45.470 --> 00:00:48.480
but mostly because it's just very hard to
measure slang.

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:52.920
In any case, though, I think that 'cool' could
give 'drunk' a run for its money.

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:57.540
Dope, rad, bad, off the heezy, the bee's knees,
on the ball, peachy keen, slammin', smokin',

00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:01.920
sweet, far out, groovy, hip, legit, boss,
wizard, wicked, jokes, choice, gnarly, the

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:06.740
cat's pajamas, crackalackin, nifty, fine,
fab, fly, fresh, phat, and on fleek.

00:01:06.740 --> 00:01:08.640
Just to name a few!

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:11.440
Culture defines the words and the words define
the culture.

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:16.670
The very existence of the concept of cool
itself requires a kind of cultural self-awareness.

00:01:16.670 --> 00:01:21.060
Culture needs to know that culture exists,
so it can identify and co-opt words to use

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:25.190
to define itself with, but I think that culture
is aware of itself in a kind of limited way.

00:01:25.190 --> 00:01:28.700
Like, when I look at myself in the mirror,
I'm seeing myself on the surface level.

00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:32.490
I can like, do my hair and check for my pimples,
but not really gettin' very deep.

00:01:32.490 --> 00:01:36.110
When I think about myself, though, I'm thinking
about my hopes and my fears and my motivations,

00:01:36.110 --> 00:01:38.481
and the effects that I have on the world around
me.

00:01:38.481 --> 00:01:41.190
That's what I'm thinking about my actual self,
and that's a whole other thing.

00:01:41.190 --> 00:01:44.890
Culture, it feels like, is kinda of often
mindlessly self-aware, like it's looking at

00:01:44.890 --> 00:01:48.680
itself in the mirror and being quite impressed
with what it sees there, but not examining

00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:50.740
itself in a deeper way.

00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:55.640
Culture has a hard time being truly self-aware,
being able to examine itself, its motivations,

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:58.010
its effects, and its hopes and its fears.

00:01:58.010 --> 00:02:02.450
It's hard for culture to do that, but one
way that we can do it is to examine the choices

00:02:02.450 --> 00:02:07.790
that culture makes, and maybe that'll give
us some deeper insight, and 'cool', that bleeding

00:02:07.790 --> 00:02:10.749
edge of the collective self, is a really good
place to look for that insight.

00:02:10.749 --> 00:02:14.480
Like in the 60s, with the focus on both space
exploration and drug culture, words for 'cool'

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:18.209
were like, 'far out', and 'out of this world'
or just 'gone'.

00:02:18.209 --> 00:02:22.680
Surfer culture was more about enthusiasm than
aloofness, and spawned words like 'mondo'

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:24.920
and 'bodacious' and 'radical'.

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:28.480
These words almost always come to the mainstream
from some kind of subculture, out of rock

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:33.639
and roll or punk or beat poets, but most commonly,
they come out of marginalized cultures.

00:02:33.639 --> 00:02:39.110
The first use of 'cool' itself to identify
the concept of coolness was traced back to

00:02:39.110 --> 00:02:41.999
the 1880s in African-American English.

00:02:41.999 --> 00:02:46.760
Often, these words are co-opted and redefined
by the mainstream in a kind of colonization

00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:51.349
of the cool of marginalized cultures that
the mainstream has itself marginalized.

00:02:51.349 --> 00:02:55.569
Diversity creates these opportunities for
cultural advancement, but the wave can also

00:02:55.569 --> 00:03:01.680
be destructive if we forget that we're the
ones who built it and don't question its effects.

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:06.069
Ultimately, and thank goodness, we get to
decide for ourselves what 'cool' is.

00:03:06.069 --> 00:03:09.049
We both create and are created by culture.

00:03:09.049 --> 00:03:12.530
So maybe we have an obligation to inspect
that wave and to see if we can understand

00:03:12.530 --> 00:03:14.629
its motivations and its effects.

00:03:14.629 --> 00:03:18.670
And that is why I have become obsessed with
the word 'cool'.

00:03:18.670 --> 00:03:20.519
John, I'll see you on Tuesday.

00:03:20.519 --> 00:03:23.909
Please, I want so badly for there to be productive
discussion in the comments.

00:03:23.909 --> 00:03:27.809
There was a lot that I couldn't say in this
video, because there's just not enough time,

00:03:27.809 --> 00:03:31.720
so I hope a lot of that discussion gets to
happen down in the comments.

00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:35.400
Also, I'm going to be performing with Welcome
